WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.380 --> 00:00:10.380
Operating systems. Once you've made an LPAR on a mainframe, you probably want to run an operating system on it.

2
00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:15.380
Now, the mainframe runs several different operating systems, and I'm going to take you through them.

3
00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:23.380
First up, there's ZOS. Now, one thing you'll notice about all the IBM built operating systems is they all start with a Z slash.

4
00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:32.380
When the mainframe moved to the Z architecture, basically when it started running 64-bit, the operating systems got renamed with that Z as well.

5
00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:39.380
So that's why you see that. So the ZOS operating system is the flagship IBM mainframe operating system.

6
00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:49.380
And if you trace the lineage of that operating system way back, it's a direct descendant of the original OS360 that ran back on that first mainframe back in 1964.

7
00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:58.380
Its focus is on running a wide variety of mixed workloads simultaneously, all while delivering an extremely high level of quality of service.

8
00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:08.380
If someone today has a mainframe, chances are they're running ZOS. And we're actually going to come back to this and talk more about ZOS later, but I want to talk about the other operating systems first.

9
00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:20.380
First, there's Z-TPF, which stands for Z transaction processing facility, commonly used by companies dealing with high volumes of transactions that need to get handled in real time.

10
00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:31.380
That means credit card processors, airline reservation systems, hotel and auto rental chains, Z-TPF is extremely tied to the world of travel and logistics.

11
00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:46.380
ZVM is all about virtualization. Now, we mentioned ZVM briefly before the idea behind it is to provide guest operating systems running essentially within ZVM, and then they get access to all the resources that the host ZVM is running with.

12
00:01:46.380 --> 00:02:00.380
Interesting thing about ZVM is that it is a type one hypervisor. So, instead of running it on top of another operating system and then using that to host the virtual machines, ZVM doesn't need another operating system to do its thing.

13
00:02:00.380 --> 00:02:11.380
It does its own thing. That makes ZVM extremely efficient and valuable for testing and development. Any operating system that runs on the mainframe will run within ZVM.

14
00:02:11.380 --> 00:02:18.380
So, if you need a whole bunch of systems, but don't want to use up your L-Pars, ZVM can do some pretty interesting things.

15
00:02:18.380 --> 00:02:35.380
The other operating system IBM makes is called ZVSE. This is a lesser used operating system, but it's still very important for smaller companies who need something like ZOS, which can do transaction and batch processing, but don't need something that big or that complex.

16
00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:42.380
Also don't need as many people to run as ZVSE instance, and that's appealing to smaller companies with fewer people.

17
00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:50.380
And then there's Linux. The same Linux that runs on phones, watches, game consoles, toasters. Guess what? It runs on the mainframe too.

18
00:02:50.380 --> 00:02:57.380
And it runs extremely well. And if you know Linux, then you know Linux on the mainframe. Everything looks pretty much the same.

19
00:02:57.380 --> 00:03:02.380
If you're not familiar with Linux, it's an open source operating system that's used pretty much all over the place.

20
00:03:02.380 --> 00:03:08.380
I remember when Linux first started showing up on the mainframe, people thought it was crazy. It was a crazy idea.

21
00:03:08.380 --> 00:03:15.380
The idea of an operating system running on our beloved mainframe that was coded by a bunch of strangers on the internet.

22
00:03:15.380 --> 00:03:22.380
I mean, you wanted to take this seriously? Well, that was like back in 2000. Another leap of faith in Linux is still running on the mainframe.

23
00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:29.380
It's hugely popular. Lastly, KVM, or a kernel-based virtual machine, is an open source hypervisor.

24
00:03:29.380 --> 00:03:37.380
Similar to ZVM, it's another type I hypervisor, meaning it doesn't require another operating system because it is an operating system.

25
00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:45.380
People like it because it integrates with KVM running on other platforms, and it's used a lot for distributed workloads where you need a lot of something.

26
00:03:45.380 --> 00:03:53.380
Because you can scale it out horizontally by adding more instances or up vertically by adding additional capacity on demand.

27
00:03:53.380 --> 00:03:57.380
All right, back to the Z operating system, often just short into ZOS.

28
00:03:57.380 --> 00:04:04.380
We're going to spend a lot of time talking about ZOS in this course because it is the premier mainframe operating system.

29
00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:13.380
It's built specifically for the Z hardware. It offers continuous high volume operations with high security and stability.

30
00:04:13.380 --> 00:04:21.380
ZOS excels at supporting a huge ecosystem of different workloads, supporting large numbers of different users at the same time.

31
00:04:21.380 --> 00:04:26.380
It does all this while maintaining the highest quality of service for any operating system.

32
00:04:26.380 --> 00:04:35.380
By better knowing the management functions of an operating system, we can better evaluate exactly what components of ZOS are responsible for what we're interested in.

33
00:04:35.380 --> 00:04:44.380
The ZOS operating system is made up of components. Some of these components are base elements, which the operating system comes with and is configured with by default.

34
00:04:44.380 --> 00:04:47.380
Other components are add-ons called features.

35
00:04:47.380 --> 00:04:53.380
The OS doesn't necessarily need these to run, but you may run into them depending on your job role.

36
00:04:53.380 --> 00:04:57.380
Now, I'll just tell you right up front, there's a lot of components.

37
00:04:57.380 --> 00:05:01.380
Some of them will just breeze right through, no need to define what's essentially spelled out in the name.

38
00:05:01.380 --> 00:05:04.380
Others, we're going to dive deeper into.

39
00:05:04.380 --> 00:05:07.380
We're going to cover a lot of these in much greater detail later.

40
00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:09.380
So, here we go.

41
00:05:09.380 --> 00:05:14.380
To start off, we've got the transaction manager, which does basically what it's called.

42
00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:18.380
It manages transactions across one or more resources.

43
00:05:18.380 --> 00:05:20.380
Then there's databases.

44
00:05:20.380 --> 00:05:27.380
The databases job is to keep data not only stored, but organized, so it can be easily found and updated later on.

45
00:05:27.380 --> 00:05:29.380
All right, let's talk about batch jobs.

46
00:05:29.380 --> 00:05:32.380
Some work doesn't need to happen in real time.

47
00:05:32.380 --> 00:05:38.380
This could be because the systems are less busy later on in the day, or it could just be because of the nature of the data.

48
00:05:38.380 --> 00:05:46.380
For example, a delivery logistics company loads its trucks at night so they can hit the roads bright and early in the morning when the drivers come in.

49
00:05:46.380 --> 00:05:53.380
They might take all the data about what deliveries need to be made that day and process it all at once, so it's ready when the drivers come in.

50
00:05:53.380 --> 00:05:56.380
And that's where you might see a batch job.

51
00:05:56.380 --> 00:05:58.380
Then there's the security manager.

52
00:05:58.380 --> 00:06:01.380
Make sure you can only get to resources if you're supposed to.

53
00:06:01.380 --> 00:06:08.380
Crypto services, that allows for the data to be protected by being encrypted and then used by decrypting it.

54
00:06:08.380 --> 00:06:10.380
There's end user interfaces.

55
00:06:10.380 --> 00:06:15.380
Obviously, you need something to bridge that gap between the system and the people who are trying to use it.

56
00:06:15.380 --> 00:06:19.380
And we use TSO, which stands for time sharing option.

57
00:06:19.380 --> 00:06:23.380
That makes it possible for one system to have thousands of connected users.

58
00:06:23.380 --> 00:06:28.380
So there's this concept on ZOS of a job, and you're going to hear that word a lot.

59
00:06:28.380 --> 00:06:34.380
A job is like a program, but it's a program with a specific set of input and output.

60
00:06:34.380 --> 00:06:37.380
So imagine you've got a payroll program, right?

61
00:06:37.380 --> 00:06:38.380
It does the same thing.

62
00:06:38.380 --> 00:06:43.380
It takes the number of hours somebody worked their wages and creates a sheet basically saying,

63
00:06:43.380 --> 00:06:46.380
pay this person this much or maybe even cuts the checks for them.

64
00:06:46.380 --> 00:06:53.380
So the program itself doesn't change from run to run, but it's got a different set of input and output each time.

65
00:06:53.380 --> 00:06:59.380
So you're going to write how you want your job to run in something called the job control language or JCL.

66
00:06:59.380 --> 00:07:03.380
And that gets submitted to the job entry subsystem, JES.

67
00:07:03.380 --> 00:07:05.380
So you win me so far?

68
00:07:05.380 --> 00:07:06.380
Good.

69
00:07:06.380 --> 00:07:12.380
When the system gets around to running it, it processes the data and it puts every piece of output.

70
00:07:12.380 --> 00:07:15.380
Good, bad, and different into the job output.

71
00:07:15.380 --> 00:07:18.380
So you know exactly how your job ran.

72
00:07:18.380 --> 00:07:26.380
When you use another component, the system display and search facility, thankfully shortened to SDSF to look at all of that output.

73
00:07:26.380 --> 00:07:35.380
Now, as you can imagine, when it comes time to upgrade and manage software on a mainframe, it's kind of important that everything get managed and tracked.

74
00:07:35.380 --> 00:07:41.380
Some software depends on other software to run, so to install, you have to also install what's called a prerequisite.

75
00:07:41.380 --> 00:07:46.380
Sometimes you can't upgrade one piece of software without also upgrading another one.

76
00:07:46.380 --> 00:07:49.380
So how do you keep all these dependencies straight?

77
00:07:49.380 --> 00:07:51.380
Fortunately, you don't have to.

78
00:07:51.380 --> 00:07:56.380
You have SMPE, the system modification program extended.

79
00:07:56.380 --> 00:07:59.380
Performance is extremely important on the mainframe.

80
00:07:59.380 --> 00:08:02.380
And it's not enough just to say, yep, everything's running really fast.

81
00:08:02.380 --> 00:08:03.380
It's good, right?

82
00:08:03.380 --> 00:08:11.380
You need to be able to say exactly how long the average transaction is running for, how long users are waiting for the response, all that kind of stuff.

83
00:08:11.380 --> 00:08:18.380
On the mainframe, you'll use RMF or the resource measurement facility to keep tabs on how everything is being used.

84
00:08:18.380 --> 00:08:25.380
This is kind of like the gauges in your car, but it also includes data gathered over time, so you can see trends and patterns.

85
00:08:25.380 --> 00:08:34.380
Managing storage is important, and the mainframe uses DFSMS, the data facility storage management service, to manage what data goes where.

86
00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:40.380
It also handles backups, migrations, copies, and all input-output management.

87
00:08:40.380 --> 00:08:45.380
Something else that's fairly unique to the mainframe platform is workload manager.

88
00:08:45.380 --> 00:08:54.380
Just like with other managers, it makes sure that when you're running multiple things at the same time on the same platform, they all get a fair slice of the system resources.

89
00:08:54.380 --> 00:08:57.380
But the workload manager takes it a step further.

90
00:08:57.380 --> 00:09:00.380
It's actually kind of backwards from what you might be used to.

91
00:09:00.380 --> 00:09:02.380
You start by defining goals.

92
00:09:02.380 --> 00:09:07.380
You say, whenever this type of transaction happens, it needs to finish in this amount of time.

93
00:09:07.380 --> 00:09:13.380
Or maybe it's just this LPAR over here is extremely important. It gets priority over all the other LPARs.

94
00:09:13.380 --> 00:09:17.380
So don't let anything they're doing slow down anything on this LPAR.

95
00:09:17.380 --> 00:09:19.380
It's the most important one.

96
00:09:19.380 --> 00:09:22.380
So when you set it up correctly, it's not a free-for-all.

97
00:09:22.380 --> 00:09:25.380
It's a carefully controlled and managed environment.

98
00:09:25.380 --> 00:09:36.380
TCPIP, or to be complete, SNA, TCPIP, which stands for Systems Network Architecture, Transmission Control Protocol, Internet Protocol.

99
00:09:36.380 --> 00:09:39.380
You can see why we just say, SNA, TCPIP.

100
00:09:39.380 --> 00:09:44.380
This is a component of ZOS, and it's where all the network communication lives.

101
00:09:44.380 --> 00:09:47.380
That's all computer to computer, computer to internet.

102
00:09:47.380 --> 00:09:51.380
Basically, anything network network gets used uses SNA, TCPIP.

103
00:09:51.380 --> 00:09:57.380
Now, whenever anything happens on the mainframe, it's extremely important that there be a record of it happening.

104
00:09:57.380 --> 00:10:02.380
The idea of a program writing out a diagnostic log isn't unique to the mainframe.

105
00:10:02.380 --> 00:10:09.380
But because there's so much happening here, it's important that there be a standard format and a facility to keep it all straight.

106
00:10:09.380 --> 00:10:15.380
Almost like a database for tracking events, that's SMF, the Systems Management Facility.

107
00:10:15.380 --> 00:10:21.380
SMF comes in handy for diagnosing problems, auditing transactions, and for tracing process flows.

108
00:10:21.380 --> 00:10:26.380
And last, but certainly not least, is USS, or UNIX system services.

109
00:10:26.380 --> 00:10:31.380
You may not know this, but you can actually run UNIX programs within the ZOS environment.

110
00:10:31.380 --> 00:10:35.380
ZOS has USS, which is a POSIX compliant environment.

111
00:10:35.380 --> 00:10:38.380
So, you can run scripts, utilities, and tools.

112
00:10:38.380 --> 00:10:42.380
It's not an operating system within ZOS, like a VM.

113
00:10:42.380 --> 00:10:44.380
It's an environment.

114
00:10:44.380 --> 00:10:47.380
So, that means a lot of the utilities are shared between them.

115
00:10:47.380 --> 00:10:51.380
And you can even fire off the ZOS jobs from within a UNIX shell.

116
00:10:51.380 --> 00:10:55.380
And those are the ZOS components.

117
00:10:55.380 --> 00:10:59.380
It took a little while, but I think it was time well spent.
